1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,034
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is Intro to Computer

2
00:00:05,034 --> 00:00:10,696
Science, Unit 1: What Is a Computer? We'll
open up the box and see how a computer

3
00:00:10,696 --> 00:00:11,605
really works.

4
00:00:12,779 --> 00:00:17,982
Phones, laptops, game consoles, even the
chip in a microwave. They look very

5
00:00:17,982 --> 00:00:18,667
different.

6
00:00:19,117 --> 00:00:21,890
But every computer does the same four
jobs.

7
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:23,599
Let's see what they are.

8
00:00:24,749 --> 00:00:29,250
Job one is input. Input is information
going into the computer.

9
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:35,047
A keyboard, a mouse, a touch screen, a
microphone and a camera are all input

10
00:00:35,047 --> 00:00:35,610
devices.

11
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:41,971
Job two is processing. The computer
follows instructions to change the input

12
00:00:41,971 --> 00:00:43,480
into something useful.

13
00:00:43,930 --> 00:00:48,559
Adding numbers, sorting a list, or
brightening a photo are all processing.

14
00:00:49,709 --> 00:00:54,274
Job three is storage. The computer keeps
data so it can use it later.

15
00:00:54,724 --> 00:00:58,948
Your photos, your saved games, and the
apps themselves are all stored.

16
00:01:00,098 --> 00:01:04,706
Job four is output. Output is information
coming out of the computer.

17
00:01:05,156 --> 00:01:10,468
A screen, speakers, a printer, and a
phone's vibration are all output devices.

18
00:01:11,618 --> 00:01:15,821
Put it together, and you get the input,
process, store, output cycle.

19
00:01:16,271 --> 00:01:20,111
Take a calculator. You press seven, plus,
five. That's input.

20
00:01:20,561 --> 00:01:25,059
It adds them. That's processing. It
remembers the answer for the next step.

21
00:01:25,059 --> 00:01:25,958
That's storage.

22
00:01:26,408 --> 00:01:28,435
And it shows twelve. That's output.

23
00:01:29,585 --> 00:01:33,980
Your turn. You take a photo with your
phone. Which job is the camera doing?

24
00:01:34,430 --> 00:01:35,710
Pause the video now.

25
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:39,893
The camera is input. It sends light, as
data, into the phone.

26
00:01:40,343 --> 00:01:44,290
Next, we'll open up the box and see the
parts that do each job.

27
00:01:45,439 --> 00:01:49,492
If you open up a computer, you'll find
three parts that matter most.

28
00:01:49,942 --> 00:01:52,374
The C P U, the memory, and the storage.

29
00:01:53,524 --> 00:01:58,474
The C P U, or central processing unit, is
the part that processes.

30
00:01:58,924 --> 00:02:04,513
It follows instructions, one tiny step at
a time, billions of times every second.

31
00:02:04,963 --> 00:02:10,609
That speed is measured in gigahertz. Three
gigahertz means about three billion steps

32
00:02:10,609 --> 00:02:11,214
a second.

33
00:02:12,364 --> 00:02:17,825
Memory, also called RAM, holds what the
computer is working on right now.

34
00:02:18,275 --> 00:02:22,200
The app you have open, and the web page
you're reading, sit in memory.

35
00:02:22,650 --> 00:02:26,746
Memory is very fast, so the C P U never
has to wait long.

36
00:02:27,896 --> 00:02:31,459
Storage holds everything else, even when
the power is off.

37
00:02:31,909 --> 00:02:35,258
That's your files, your photos, and all
your installed apps.

38
00:02:35,708 --> 00:02:41,639
Storage is a hard drive or a solid state
drive, and it holds much more than memory.

39
00:02:42,789 --> 00:02:46,352
Here's a way to picture it. Think of a
student doing homework.

40
00:02:46,802 --> 00:02:49,426
The student is the C P U, doing the work.

41
00:02:49,876 --> 00:02:53,929
The desk is memory: small, but everything
on it is right at hand.

42
00:02:54,379 --> 00:02:58,923
The backpack is storage: it holds much
more, but it's slower to reach into.

43
00:03:00,073 --> 00:03:03,294
All three are connected on a board called
the motherboard.

44
00:03:03,744 --> 00:03:09,504
Data moves between them along tiny wires,
again and again, as you use the computer.

45
00:03:10,654 --> 00:03:16,064
Your turn. You open a document you saved
last week. Where was it while the computer

46
00:03:16,064 --> 00:03:16,585
was off?

47
00:03:17,035 --> 00:03:18,315
Pause the video now.

48
00:03:18,765 --> 00:03:24,306
It was in storage. When you open it, a
copy moves into memory so the C P U can

49
00:03:24,306 --> 00:03:25,229
work with it.

50
00:03:25,679 --> 00:03:29,583
Next, we'll see how the C P U actually
runs an instruction.

51
00:03:30,733 --> 00:03:36,728
A program is a list of instructions. But
how does the C P U actually follow them?

52
00:03:37,178 --> 00:03:40,250
It repeats a simple three step loop, over
and over.

53
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:46,029
Step one is fetch. The C P U gets the next
instruction from memory.

54
00:03:46,479 --> 00:03:51,834
A small counter inside the C P U keeps
track of which instruction comes next.

55
00:03:52,984 --> 00:03:57,762
Step two is decode. The C P U works out
what the instruction means.

56
00:03:58,212 --> 00:04:01,924
Is it asking to add? To compare? To copy a
number somewhere?

57
00:04:03,074 --> 00:04:07,341
Step three is execute. The C P U carries
out the instruction.

58
00:04:07,791 --> 00:04:12,228
Then the counter moves on, and the loop
starts again with the next instruction.

59
00:04:13,378 --> 00:04:17,837
Let's watch a tiny program. Load five. Add
three. Store the result.

60
00:04:18,287 --> 00:04:24,825
Fetch: load five. Decode: it's a load.
Execute: five goes into a register, a tiny

61
00:04:24,825 --> 00:04:27,247
storage spot inside the C P U.

62
00:04:27,697 --> 00:04:32,966
Fetch: add three. Decode: it's an add.
Execute: the register now holds eight.

63
00:04:33,416 --> 00:04:38,451
Fetch: store. Decode: it's a store.
Execute: eight is copied back to memory.

64
00:04:39,601 --> 00:04:45,404
Each loop is called a cycle. A modern C P
U does billions of cycles a second.

65
00:04:45,854 --> 00:04:51,751
Many C P Us also have several cores. Each
core runs its own fetch, decode, execute

66
00:04:51,751 --> 00:04:55,347
loop, so they can work on different tasks
at once.

67
00:04:56,497 --> 00:04:59,868
Your turn. Put these in order: execute,
fetch, decode.

68
00:05:00,318 --> 00:05:01,598
Pause the video now.

69
00:05:02,048 --> 00:05:05,461
The order is fetch, decode, execute. Then
it repeats.

70
00:05:05,911 --> 00:05:10,178
Next, we'll find out why memory forgets
everything when the power goes off.

71
00:05:11,328 --> 00:05:16,209
You're writing an essay, and the power
goes out. When the computer comes back,

72
00:05:16,209 --> 00:05:17,899
your last changes are gone.

73
00:05:18,349 --> 00:05:21,634
But the files you saved yesterday are
fine. Why?

74
00:05:22,784 --> 00:05:27,776
Memory, or RAM, is volatile. That means it
needs power to keep its data.

75
00:05:28,226 --> 00:05:34,383
RAM stores each bit as a tiny electric
charge. When the power stops, the charges

76
00:05:34,383 --> 00:05:36,461
fade, and the data is gone.

77
00:05:37,611 --> 00:05:41,877
Storage is non volatile. It keeps data
with no power at all.

78
00:05:42,327 --> 00:05:48,189
A solid state drive traps charge in cells
that hold it for years. A hard drive

79
00:05:48,189 --> 00:05:52,247
stores bits as tiny magnetic spots on a
spinning disk.

80
00:05:53,397 --> 00:05:56,320
So why not use storage for everything?
Speed.

81
00:05:56,770 --> 00:06:02,823
RAM can be about a hundred times faster
than a solid state drive, and thousands of

82
00:06:02,823 --> 00:06:05,111
times faster than a hard drive.

83
00:06:05,561 --> 00:06:11,917
And why not use RAM for everything? Cost.
RAM costs much more per gigabyte, and it

84
00:06:11,917 --> 00:06:12,537
forgets.

85
00:06:13,687 --> 00:06:15,501
That's why computers use both.

86
00:06:15,951 --> 00:06:21,382
A typical laptop might have sixteen
gigabytes of RAM, and five hundred twelve

87
00:06:21,382 --> 00:06:22,863
gigabytes of storage.

88
00:06:23,313 --> 00:06:29,030
Programs and files live in storage, and
get copied into RAM while you use them.

89
00:06:30,180 --> 00:06:32,228
This is also why saving matters.

90
00:06:32,678 --> 00:06:38,027
When you press save, the computer copies
your work from RAM to storage, where it's

91
00:06:38,027 --> 00:06:38,353
safe.

92
00:06:39,503 --> 00:06:43,556
Your turn. Which one is volatile: RAM, or
a solid state drive?

93
00:06:44,006 --> 00:06:45,286
Pause the video now.

94
00:06:45,736 --> 00:06:50,024
RAM is volatile. It loses its data when
the power is off.

95
00:06:50,474 --> 00:06:55,807
Next, we'll meet the program that runs all
the other programs: the operating system.

96
00:06:56,957 --> 00:07:01,138
When you turn on a computer, one program
starts before all the others.

97
00:07:01,588 --> 00:07:07,761
It's the operating system. Windows, mac O
S, Linux, Android and i O S are all

98
00:07:07,761 --> 00:07:09,204
operating systems.

99
00:07:10,354 --> 00:07:13,981
The operating system sits between your
apps and the hardware.

100
00:07:14,431 --> 00:07:20,577
Apps don't talk to the C P U, memory, or
storage directly. They ask the operating

101
00:07:20,577 --> 00:07:21,108
system.

102
00:07:22,258 --> 00:07:24,520
Job one: managing the C P U.

103
00:07:24,970 --> 00:07:30,728
You might have music, a browser and a game
running at once. The operating system

104
00:07:30,728 --> 00:07:36,343
switches the C P U between them, so fast
that they all seem to run at the same

105
00:07:36,343 --> 00:07:36,703
time.

106
00:07:37,853 --> 00:07:39,645
Job two: managing memory.

107
00:07:40,095 --> 00:07:45,524
The operating system gives each app its
own space in RAM, and keeps apps from

108
00:07:45,524 --> 00:07:47,498
reading each other's memory.

109
00:07:48,648 --> 00:07:51,272
Job three: managing files and devices.

110
00:07:51,722 --> 00:07:56,903
It organizes storage into files and
folders, and it talks to the keyboard, the

111
00:07:56,903 --> 00:07:59,295
screen, the printer and the network.

112
00:08:00,445 --> 00:08:03,240
Job four: security and the user interface.

113
00:08:03,690 --> 00:08:09,047
It handles logins and permissions, like
whether an app may use your camera. And it

114
00:08:09,047 --> 00:08:13,098
draws the desktop, the windows and the
home screen you tap on.

115
00:08:14,248 --> 00:08:20,098
Your turn. Two apps are open, and only one
C P U core. Which part of the computer

116
00:08:20,098 --> 00:08:22,120
decides which app runs next?

117
00:08:22,570 --> 00:08:23,850
Pause the video now.

118
00:08:24,300 --> 00:08:28,396
The operating system. It schedules the C P
U between apps.

119
00:08:28,846 --> 00:08:32,472
Next, we'll see what software is, and how
programs get made.

120
00:08:33,623 --> 00:08:36,588
Hardware is the parts of a computer you
can touch.

121
00:08:37,038 --> 00:08:40,558
Software is the instructions that tell the
hardware what to do.

122
00:08:41,708 --> 00:08:43,756
There are two main kinds of software.

123
00:08:44,206 --> 00:08:49,547
System software runs the computer itself,
like the operating system and device

124
00:08:49,547 --> 00:08:50,094
drivers.

125
00:08:50,544 --> 00:08:55,834
Application software, or apps, does jobs
for you: a browser, a game, a word

126
00:08:55,834 --> 00:08:56,539
processor.

127
00:08:57,689 --> 00:09:03,236
Every program starts as source code: text
written by a person in a programming

128
00:09:03,236 --> 00:09:05,369
language, like Python or Java.

129
00:09:05,819 --> 00:09:09,744
Here's one line of Python. It tells the
computer to print hello.

130
00:09:10,894 --> 00:09:16,446
But the C P U only understands machine
code: instructions written as binary

131
00:09:16,446 --> 00:09:17,038
numbers.

132
00:09:17,488 --> 00:09:19,771
So source code has to be translated.

133
00:09:20,921 --> 00:09:22,628
There are two ways to translate.

134
00:09:23,078 --> 00:09:29,200
A compiler translates the whole program
into machine code before it runs. C and C

135
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:31,014
plus plus work this way.

136
00:09:31,464 --> 00:09:37,278
An interpreter translates and runs the
program one line at a time. Python usually

137
00:09:37,278 --> 00:09:38,354
works this way.

138
00:09:39,504 --> 00:09:45,129
Real programs are built step by step.
Plan, write the code, test it, and fix the

139
00:09:45,129 --> 00:09:46,395
bugs. Then repeat.

140
00:09:46,845 --> 00:09:51,782
That cycle is how every app you use was
made, and it's how you'll write programs

141
00:09:51,782 --> 00:09:52,029
too.

142
00:09:53,179 --> 00:09:57,894
Your turn. Is a web browser system
software, or application software?

143
00:09:58,344 --> 00:09:59,624
Pause the video now.

144
00:10:00,074 --> 00:10:03,188
It's application software. It does a job
for you.

145
00:10:03,638 --> 00:10:08,808
That's the end of Unit one. Next, we'll
start Unit two: how computers store

146
00:10:08,808 --> 00:10:10,806
everything as ones and zeros.

147
00:10:11,956 --> 00:10:16,633
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

148
00:10:16,633 --> 00:10:17,908
miss the next lesson.

149
00:10:18,358 --> 00:10:19,254
See you there.

